eneral early-Advance permission.','Existing campaign test acknowledges application before allowing campaign; its helper behavior must be examined before interpreting it as a configuration-completion requirement.'],unknowns=['Does early-Advance permission cover membership-bearing batches or only ordinary commands/snapshots?','Does a source-backed legal API history reach campaigning before the actual membership callback despite all caller requirements?','If reachable, is the responsibility local campaign eligibility or only a broader consensus property requiring an additional producer history?'],priority=3)\nx=dict(action='continue',rationale='Establish the usable both-core map and select the concrete application-progress/authority premise without asserting a defect.',map_path='map.json',sources=sources,question=q,feedback=dict(ref_ids=['b-application','b-authority','b-reads','s-node-completion-contract','s-node-read-contract','s-election-entry'],answered='Completed source tracing for the representative formation/context backbone. The pending-read observation has a nontrivial protective premise: with completion-before-Advance, unapplied committed configurations block election, while committing previously uncommitted old configuration entries first requires a current-term leader commit that already releases reads. Node also explicitly allows silent ReadIndex loss, so an omitted release alone cannot establish bounded liveness. Early Advance is now the narrower authority premise to investigate; it can update the applied cursor independently of actual ApplyConfChange. This is a contract/history question, not a finding.',remaining=['Resolve early-Advance applicability and inspect the existing test helper before constructing a controlled schedule.','Preserve synchronous persistence-order contract conflict as an independent unmapped responsibility surface.'],understanding='updated',rationale='Map facts preserve acknowledged progress rather than assuming completed membership 